      "context": "Mei investigates an enzyme that converts a substrate into a product. She measures initial reaction rates at two substrate concentrations. All reactions contain the same amount of enzyme. When present, inhibitor X has a concentration of 1 mM.\n\n| Temperature (°C) | pH | Inhibitor X | Substrate (2 mM): initial rate (µmol/min) | Substrate (20 mM): initial rate (µmol/min) |\n|---:|---:|---|---:|---:|\n| 37 | 7 | Absent | 40 | 87 |\n| 60 | 7 | Absent | 8 | 18 |\n| 37 | 4 | Absent | 10 | 22 |\n| 37 | 7 | Present | 10 | 53 |\n\n",
      "question": "(a) Identify the two conditions Mei should compare to determine the effect of pH at 2 mM substrate. Calculate the decrease in initial rate between those conditions.\n\n(b) Propose a molecular explanation for the lower enzyme activity at 60°C and a molecular explanation for the lower activity at pH 4.\n\n(c) Identify a type of inhibition consistent with the data for inhibitor X. Justify your identification using the effect of increasing substrate concentration on inhibition.\n\n(d) Design an experiment to determine whether the loss of activity after exposure to 60°C is reversible when the enzyme is returned to 37°C. Include a control and describe the results expected if the loss is reversible.",
      "rubric": [
        {
          "criterion": "(a) Comparison",
          "description": "Identifies the reactions at 37°C without inhibitor X, at pH 7 and pH 4.",
          "points": 1
        },
        {
          "criterion": "(a) Calculation",
          "description": "Calculates a decrease of 30 µmol/min.",
          "points": 1
        },
        {
          "criterion": "(b) Temperature",
          "description": "Explains that high temperature can disrupt interactions maintaining the enzyme's shape, altering the active site and reducing activity.",
          "points": 1
        },
        {
          "criterion": "(b) pH",
          "description": "Explains that a change in pH can alter the ionization of amino acid side chains, disrupting enzyme shape or substrate binding and reducing activity.",
          "points": 1
        },
        {
          "criterion": "(c) Inhibition",
          "description": "Identifies competitive inhibition as consistent with the data.",
          "points": 1
        },
        {
          "criterion": "(c) Justification",
          "description": "Explains that the inhibitor reduces the rate by a smaller proportion at 20 mM substrate than at 2 mM substrate, consistent with substrate competing with the inhibitor.",
          "points": 1
        },
        {
          "criterion": "(d) Experimental design",
          "description": "Describes exposing enzyme to 60°C, returning it to 37°C, and measuring its activity alongside an otherwise identically treated enzyme sample held at 37°C.",
          "points": 1
        },
        {
          "criterion": "(d) Expected result",
          "description": "States that reversible loss would be supported if the heat-exposed enzyme regained activity comparable to the control after returning to 37°C.",
          "points": 1
        }
      ],
      "keyPoints": [
        "The pH comparison holds temperature, inhibitor condition, and substrate concentration constant.",
        "Temperature and pH can reduce activity by affecting interactions or charges important to enzyme function.",
        "Increasing substrate concentration reduces the proportional effect of inhibitor X, consistent with competitive inhibition.",
        "Activity measured after cooling can distinguish recovery from persistent loss under the tested conditions."
      ],
      "sampleOutline": "Compare the pH 7 and pH 4 reactions at 37°C without inhibitor. The rate decreases by 30 µmol/min. Heat can disrupt interactions that maintain the active site's shape; altered pH can change side-chain charges and affect shape or binding. Competitive inhibition is consistent with the inhibitor's smaller proportional effect at high substrate concentration. Preincubate one enzyme sample at 60°C and a control at 37°C for the same time, then assay both at 37°C under identical conditions. Similar activities would support reversible loss."
